---

Typeface/Off's language catalog (see docs/languages.md) is derived from
Hyperglot.

    Hyperglot — https://github.com/rosettatype/hyperglot
    Developed by Rosetta Type/Research and the Hyperglot contributors.
    Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0 —
    https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0

    Hyperglot contributors — see CONTRIBUTORS.txt in the Hyperglot distribution.

Typeface/Off expands inventory inheritance, retains preliminary and verified
language records with primary or historical orthographies, and converts the
data to JSON. The derived catalog is bundled as
Sources/TypefaceCore/Resources/languages.json, generated by `make languages`.
Per-language contributors, reviewers, sources, and notes are retained. The
upstream license is bundled verbatim as
Sources/TypefaceCore/Resources/Hyperglot-LICENSE.

---

Typeface/Off's character-frequency weighting (see docs/character-frequency.md)
is derived from three third-party datasets, described below. None of the
datasets themselves is redistributed; only the derived per-character shares
are bundled, as Sources/TypefaceCore/Resources/character-frequencies.tsv,
generated from them by `make frequencies`.

---

The per-language character counts are FineFreq, a table of per-code-point
frequency counts computed over the FineWeb and FineWeb2 web corpora
(themselves built from Common Crawl).

    FineWeb and FineWeb2 — Hugging Face and the dataset contributors.
    https://huggingface.co/datasets/HuggingFaceFW/fineweb
    https://huggingface.co/datasets/HuggingFaceFW/fineweb-2
    Open Data Commons Attribution License 1.0 —
    https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/by/1-0/
    Common Crawl — https://commoncrawl.org

    FineFreq — https://huggingface.co/datasets/lgi2p/finefreq
    © Binbin Xu, licensed under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International
    (CC BY 4.0) — https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

    Binbin Xu. FineFreq: A Multilingual Character Frequency Dataset from
    Web-Scale Text. arXiv:2512.09701 (2025).

Typeface/Off normalizes each of FineFreq's per-language tables, corrects them
for the characters NFKC normalization folded away (see below), mixes them by
each language's Ethnologue speaker count (see below), and keeps only
characters with a share of at least 0.001%. The dataset itself is not
redistributed; only that derived mixture is bundled.

---

The characters that FineFreq's NFKC normalization folds away (for example …,
½, ™, and ，) are instead estimated from the Leipzig Corpora Collection,
whose non-normalized text preserves them.

    Leipzig Corpora Collection — https://wortschatz.uni-leipzig.de
    © Universität Leipzig, licensed under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0
    International (CC BY 4.0) — https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
    Terms for the downloadable text corpora — https://wortschatz-leipzig.de/en/usage

    D. Goldhahn, T. Eckart & U. Quasthoff. Building Large Monolingual
    Dictionaries at the Leipzig Corpora Collection: From 100 to 200 Languages.
    Proceedings of the 8th International Conference on Language Resources and
    Evaluation (LREC 2012).

Only per-character shares derived from these corpora, calibrated onto
FineFreq's scale and then un-folded back onto the characters NFKC removed,
are bundled; the corpora themselves are not redistributed.

---

Each language's contribution to the mix is weighted by its speakers
(L1 + L2, or L1 alone for Greek and Polish), from Ethnologue.

    Ethnologue: Languages of the World, 29th edition (2026), SIL Global —
    https://www.ethnologue.com

    As reproduced in Wikipedia, "List of languages by total number of
    speakers" —
    https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_languages_by_total_number_of_speakers

    Greek, Hebrew, and Polish from Ethnologue's 22nd edition (2019), as
    recorded in Wikidata — https://www.wikidata.org

Only the speaker counts used as mixing weights are reflected in the
generated table; no Ethnologue, Wikipedia, or Wikidata text is redistributed.

